<h2>The constitutional baseline</h2>
<p>Article 20(3) of the Constitution says that no person accused of an offence shall be compelled to be a witness against himself. The guarantee is against <em>testimonial</em> compulsion: being made to convey information that resides in one&rsquo;s mind. It does not cover the body as a source of identification material. A handwriting specimen is compared, not read for its meaning; a voice sample is matched for its acoustics, not its content; a blood sample yields a profile, not a confession. That distinction — communication versus physical characteristics — explains the entire statutory scheme.</p>

<h2>The three statutory routes</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>What is sought</th><th>Provision</th><th>Who orders</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Specimen signature, handwriting, finger impressions, voice sample</td><td>Section 349, BNSS</td><td>Magistrate, on application during investigation or proceeding</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Medical examination of an arrested accused, including blood, swabs and material for DNA analysis</td><td>Sections 51&ndash;53, BNSS</td><td>Police request to a registered medical practitioner; reasonable force permitted</td></tr>
  <tr><td>&ldquo;Measurements&rdquo; — finger and palm impressions, photographs, iris and retina scans, physical and biological samples, behavioural attributes including signatures and handwriting</td><td>Criminal Procedure (Identification) Act, 2022</td><td>Police and prison officers for convicts and arrestees; a Magistrate may direct any person to give measurements</td></tr>
</table>
<p>Section 349 of the BNSS carries forward the old Section 311A CrPC and widens it in two ways that matter in practice. First, voice samples are now expressly within the Magistrate&rsquo;s power, placing on a statutory footing what earlier rested on judicial interpretation. Second, while the ordinary rule remains that the person should have been arrested at some stage in connection with the matter, the section empowers the Magistrate to direct even an unarrested person to give samples, for reasons recorded in writing — a significant change for investigations in which suspects are summoned rather than arrested.</p>

<h2>Where refusal leads</h2>
<p>Refusing a lawful direction is rarely a neutral act. Under the Identification Act, resisting or refusing to allow measurements is treated as obstruction of a public servant in the discharge of duty, which is itself an offence. Where a person declines to furnish a sample that a court could lawfully compel, courts may in appropriate cases draw an adverse inference from the refusal — a principle that has particular bite in paternity and matrimonial disputes involving DNA, where the choice is framed as compliance or presumption, not compliance or jail. And at the investigation stage, obstruction colours the way a court views conduct when bail or custody questions arise.</p>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Can be compelled.</strong> Fingerprints, handwriting and signature specimens, voice exemplars, photographs, blood and buccal swabs, hair, measurements, presence at a test identification parade.</div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Cannot be compelled.</strong> Confessions and self-incriminating statements, narco-analysis, polygraph and similar mind-probing techniques without consent, production of knowledge that exists only in the accused&rsquo;s memory.</div>
</div>

<h2>Safeguards worth insisting on</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>A judicial order where the statute requires one: samples under Section 349 BNSS need a Magistrate&rsquo;s direction, and for an unarrested person the order must record reasons. An omnibus police demand is not a substitute.</p>
  <p>Proper collection and sealing: the evidentiary worth of a sample depends on the integrity of its collection, sealing and transmission to the laboratory. Defence scrutiny of this chain frequently decides trials, and bail courts too have treated documented lapses as significant.</p>
  <p>Proportionality in scope: an order should specify what is to be taken and for comparison with what. Fishing expeditions across unrelated FIRs deserve objection.</p>
  <p>Medical examination of a woman, wherever the law requires it, must be by or under the supervision of a female registered medical practitioner.</p>
</div>
<p>For the accused, the practical advice is usually to comply under protest where the order is lawful, and to reserve the challenge for the forum that matters: the admissibility and weight of the resulting report at trial. For complainants and investigators, the lesson is symmetrical — the power exists, but it is only as strong as the paperwork that accompanies its exercise.</p>
<div class="note">This article is a general explanation of the statutory framework on compelled samples in criminal cases. It is not legal advice; the application of these provisions turns on the facts and the stage of each individual matter.</div>
